---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/postscript-vs-smsbump';
const title = 'Postscript vs SMSBump: Shopify Continuity Decisions (2026)';
const desc = 'SMSBump is retired. Evaluate Postscript conditionally and test permission, purchase exits, queue retirement and replacement economics.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can I still choose SMSBump for a new Shopify SMS program?",
    "answer": "No. The current Yotpo SMS notice says the application is decommissioned and its dashboard and background services are inactive. Treat SMSBump as a historical continuity question."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does that mean Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty are retired?",
    "answer": "No. Reviews and Loyalty are separate products. Confirm any replacement messaging integration, permission scope and event delivery independently."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Postscript a conditional fit for former SMSBump merchants?",
    "answer": "Yes, if an account pilot proves the required SMS targeting, purchase exits, suppression, inventory checks and operating handoff. A former supplier relationship does not establish readiness."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Postscript now offer email?",
    "answer": "Postscript has announced Email. Its Email FAQ still describes waitlist and cohort onboarding, so confirm account access, supported flows and migration scope before relying on it."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I export my old SMSBump account now?",
    "answer": "Do not assume access or portability. Inventory records already lawfully held and ask Yotpo what remains available for that account. A historical migration article is no guarantee of current recovery."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I add Sequenzy automatically?",
    "answer": "No. Evaluate an email provider only for an uncovered job. Sequenzy can be a candidate if permission, event handling, support and economics pass the same acceptance tests."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should recent purchases affect sends?",
    "answer": "Use authoritative order state to stop obsolete cart, scarcity and offer messages before dispatch. Test late and duplicate events, identity conflicts and cross-channel queues."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do I compare costs with a retired supplier?",
    "answer": "Use historical costs only as a clearly dated baseline. Compare current replacement quotes over matched eligible customers, time windows, returns and operating costs; do not request a new SMSBump plan."
  },
  {
    "question": "What makes a continuity pilot ready?",
    "answer": "Approve evidence for permission, identity, purchase exits, retries, queue retirement, ownership and rollback. Unresolved permission or order state keeps affected messages paused."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><span>Postscript vs SMSBump</span></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="badge">Historical continuity comparison · checked November 25, 2026</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Postscript vs SMSBump for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">SMSBump is a retired supplier, so this decision is about continuity and a replacement program. Postscript is a candidate when your account can demonstrate the required SMS workflows and your team can operate them safely.</p>
    </header>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
      <h2>What changed, and what remains a decision</h2>
      <p>The <a href="https://smsbump.com/pages/smsbump-vs-postscript">current SMSBump lifecycle notice</a> says the Yotpo SMS application is decommissioned, including its dashboard and background services. Yotpo's <a href="https://www.yotpo.com/blog/email-sms-migration-partners/">September 2025 migration announcement</a> scheduled Email and SMS deprecation for December 31, 2025. That historical advice does not promise current account access, exports or recoverable records.</p>
      <p><a href="https://www.yotpo.com/">Yotpo Reviews and Loyalty</a> are separate from retired Email and SMS. Keeping them can be reasonable if the replacement messaging integration passes event, identity and permission tests. Their presence does not establish a working SMS trigger or require any particular replacement.</p>
      <p><a href="https://postscript.io/launch">Postscript has announced Email</a>; its <a href="https://postscript.io/email">Email page</a> still describes waitlist and cohort onboarding. Confirm actual account access, supported workflows, commercial terms and migration responsibilities. Evaluate available SMS now and approve any email dependency only after access is demonstrated.</p>
      <h2>Conditional fits for the next program</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Positive fit</th><th>Acceptance evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Postscript SMS</td><td>A candidate for a Shopify drop or lifecycle program</td><td>Account tests prove targeting, purchase exits, retry handling and operator ownership</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Historical SMSBump continuity</td><td>Use lawful records already held to reconcile an earlier program</td><td>Document provenance, gaps and suppression; ask Yotpo about unresolved account records without assuming recovery</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Keep Reviews or Loyalty</td><td>Retain a useful separate product</td><td>Verify current integration, eligibility and event contracts with the chosen sender</td></tr>
        <tr><td>One provider for email and SMS</td><td>Useful when available features and team ownership pass a combined pilot</td><td>Demonstrated account access and shared suppression, with a documented fallback</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Separate email provider</td><td>Useful for an uncovered education or retention job</td><td>Test permissions and collisions; <a href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> is one candidate, not a required companion</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <h2>Fourteen proposed merchant acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>Every merchant below is fictional. These are proposed tests, not customer stories, measured outcomes or supplier feature guarantees. The numbers are test inputs only. Each fixture must be implemented and evidenced in the chosen account before it supports a fit decision.</p>
      <h3>Peak Trail Gear — clearance by value segment</h3>
      <p>Propose clearance eligibility from a reviewed value segment while excluding full-price loyalists. Seed a recent purchaser and an ambiguous profile; both must be held out. Compare a restrained offer with a matched holdout before approving broader reach.</p>
      <h3>Sauce Lab — batch scarcity</h3>
      <p>Propose one launch message and at most one scarcity reminder for a batch. Test a stale stock count, sold-out batch and duplicate threshold event; none may create a false scarcity claim or another send.</p>
      <h3>Ember Ceramics — fair kiln waitlist</h3>
      <p>Propose an 80-piece kiln release against a fictional 4,200-person waitlist. Preserve documented queue order, suppress purchasers and test oversubscription; an invitation is not a reservation. Email storytelling is optional and separately permissioned.</p>
      <h3>Ridge Run Coffee — subscription exclusions</h3>
      <p>Propose flash offers only for eligible non-subscribers. Test delayed subscription renewal and cancellation events against current subscription truth; hold uncertain profiles and prevent an already queued promotional send from escaping after renewal.</p>
      <h3>Cipher Audio — full-price launch</h3>
      <p>Propose an educational teaser followed by a launch text only to permissioned, engaged prospects. Test a purchase between selection and dispatch, no-discount copy approval and a conflicting identity link. Reject stale audiences rather than inventing margin gains.</p>
      <h3>Patchwork Denim — one-of-one preference targeting</h3>
      <p>Propose listing alerts to explicit style preferences. Test a sold one-of-one item, two profiles sharing a number and a repeated listing webhook. Require identity review and item availability before dispatch; do not infer preference from consent alone.</p>
      <h3>Arena Energy — collaboration offer separation</h3>
      <p>Propose a flavor-drop timing text and a separately approved email gift offer. Test eligibility for the collaboration, redemption limits and a purchase between channels. Require one offer owner and no duplicated incentive claim.</p>
      <h3>Loom & Frame — edition progress</h3>
      <p>Propose a reminder when a disclosed print edition reaches a verified threshold. Test refunds, inventory corrections and repeated threshold crossings; permit only the approved reminder and never imply unverified sell-through.</p>
      <h3>Driftwood Surf Co — seasonal restock</h3>
      <p>Propose restock alerts to the relevant waitlist with an optional swell-content calendar. Test variant availability and a purchase after queueing. A content schedule cannot override an unavailable size or an opt-out.</p>
      <h3>Noir Collective — VIP early access</h3>
      <p>Propose a Thursday release with a two-hour VIP window. Test recipient timezones, approved frequency limits, expired access links and a late retry after the window. Expired jobs must retire rather than launch into the general release.</p>
      <h3>SoleDrop ATX — size-run priority</h3>
      <p>Propose waitlist priority by requested sneaker size. Test simultaneous checkout, stock exhaustion and queue replay. Preserve priority without promising allocation; stale reservations and repeated events must not generate extra invitations.</p>
      <h3>Festival Threads Co — city and venue timing</h3>
      <p>Propose city-specific tour notices with a proposed maximum of three texts per city. Test ambiguous geography, changed venues, daylight-saving transitions and postponed dates. Require approved location and timing; cancelled event queues must retire.</p>
      <h3>Vinyl Vault Records — collector access</h3>
      <p>Propose access for waitlisted collectors meeting an approved order-value rule. Test cancelled and refunded orders, merged profiles and opt-outs; historical spend does not create permission. Document whether current or historical value drives eligibility.</p>
      <h3>Bloom & Wick — gift shipping deadlines</h3>
      <p>Propose separate gift and self-purchase journeys with verified shipping cutoffs. Test destination-specific deadlines, late fulfillment updates and a completed gift purchase. Retire obsolete urgency and approve the education channel separately.</p>
      <h2>Permission and identity are release gates</h2>
      <p>Inventory lawfully retained records: capture source, time, disclosure, channel scope, withdrawal and suppression history. A phone number, order, review or loyalty membership alone is not messaging permission. Missing proof keeps that recipient out of the pilot; do not manufacture consent or assume the retired dashboard can supply it. Ask qualified counsel about the rules applicable to the program instead of treating software defaults as legal approval.</p>
      <p>Keep email and SMS permissions distinct. Link Shopify customer IDs, phone numbers and email addresses using a documented identity policy; quarantine shared numbers, conflicting joins and unexplained merges. Test a withdrawal after audience selection and immediately before dispatch, including across providers. Apply the relevant channel withdrawal and any broader request, without assuming one channel's opt-out means every channel's status is identical.</p>
      <h2>Order truth, purchase exits and queue retirement</h2>
      <p>Assign an authoritative Shopify order state and subscription or inventory owner. Recheck eligibility before sending, including late purchases, cancellations, refunds and out-of-order events. Purchase exits must cancel obsolete cart and offer jobs across both channels. Measure selection-to-dispatch lag and fail closed for unresolved state rather than relying on an assumed refresh interval.</p>
      <p>For historical continuity, inventory every old scheduler, app, webhook and queued job your team can verify. Retire obsolete triggers and record evidence; the supplier's shutdown does not establish the state of merchant-owned jobs. If a system is inaccessible, record the uncertainty and prevent new dispatch from depending on it. Never promise to reactivate retired SMSBump as a fallback.</p>
      <p>Propose an idempotency key using event, customer, message purpose and release window. Replay duplicate webhooks, retry a timeout after provider acceptance, and reconcile dispatch logs before retrying uncertain delivery. Enforce expiry, shared frequency policy, inventory checks and purchaser suppression on retries as well as first attempts. Acceptance means one intended message, or a documented safe hold.</p>
      <h2>Approvals, handoff and rollback</h2>
      <p>Name owners for permission review, identity resolution, inventory and orders, copy and incentives, dispatch and support. Require a second approver for a release audience, cutoff and offer; record the evidence and a pause authority. Hand off the queue ledger, suppression reconciliation, open issues, support escalation and the next review time. Demonstrate STOP and HELP handling and approved recipient-timezone controls in the candidate account.</p>
      <p>Release a small eligible cohort only after the fixtures pass. Rollback means pause new sends, cancel affected queues, preserve opt-outs and reconcile accepted sends before any retry. Route unresolved replies to the named support owner. Keep an independently approved available channel only if its permission and state are verified; otherwise pause the affected journey. Resume through a fresh approval after correcting the cause, without replaying an expired promotion.</p>
      <h2>Matched hypothetical economics</h2>
      <p>Do not compare a new Postscript quote with an invented current SMSBump price. Retain dated historical costs as context, then compare current replacement quotes over the same eligible customers, geography, message mix, send window and order observation window. Include base fees, message segments and carrier charges, integration work, labor and support. Treat email access and any additional provider as explicit scope choices.</p>
      <p>Illustrative arithmetic only: two randomized groups each contain 1,000 eligible customers. Treatment net sales after discounts and returns are $6,000; control net sales on the same basis are $5,000. If incremental goods and fulfillment cost $400, messaging $100, allocated platform cost $100 and operating labor $100, incremental contribution is $1,000 − $400 − $100 − $100 − $100 = $300, or $0.30 per eligible treatment customer. These inputs are fictional and make no supplier performance claim.</p>
      <p>Discounts and returns are already in net sales; do not subtract them twice. For unequal groups, compare contribution per eligible customer using matched cost allocation and a complete returns window. Include setup cost separately or amortize consistently. Re-run at peak volume and under higher fees or weaker lift; attributed platform revenue alone does not demonstrate incremental profit.</p>
      <h2>Decision and evidence limits</h2>
      <p>Choose Postscript if the account evidence and economics fit the jobs above. Retain useful separate Yotpo products if integrations pass. Choose a different currently available sender when its pilot better meets the same requirements. No supplier gets an automatic rank, and no email pairing is mandatory.</p>
      <p>This is a public-source continuity assessment, not an account pilot or assurance of recovered data. Historical migration material cannot establish current export rights, record retention or background-service availability. Review the <a href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a>, <a href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> and <a href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint guide</a> alongside account-specific evidence.</p>
    </article>
    <div class="mt-12"><FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs SMSBump FAQ" /></div>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
